# Civil Rights Data Collection. Data Snapshot: College and Career Readiness. Issue Brief No. 3

**Authors:** 

## Abstract
The Civil Rights Data Collection (CRDC) collects a variety of information including student enrollment and educational programs and services, most of which is disaggregated by race/ethnicity, sex, limited English proficiency, and disability. The CRDC is a longstanding and important aspect of the Department of Education's Office for Civil Rights (OCR) overall strategy for administering and enforcing the civil rights statutes for which it is responsible. Information collected by the CRDC is also used by other Department of Education offices as well as policymakers and researchers outside of the Department of Education. This data snapshot highlights statistical information pertaining to the following aspects of college and career readiness of students. They include: (1) Limited access to high-level math and science courses; (2) Significant lack of access to other core courses; (3) Even less access for black, Latino, American Indian, and Alaska Native students; (4) Growing opportunity gap in gifted and talented education; (5) Advanced Placement (AP) course enrollment and testing; (6) Less access to English language instruction programs for American Indian and Native-Alaskan English learners; and (7) Higher rates of retention for students of color, English learners, and students with disabilities.

**Source:** https://eric.ed.gov/?id=ED577784

